ESP32 VectorGame Console 🚀
Version 0.1 beta – Released January 2026
This project transforms any vintage CRT oscilloscope into a functional vector gaming console. Instead of rendering pixels on a modern display, the ESP32 controls the electron beam of an oscilloscope directly via its internal Digital-to-Analog Converters (DACs).
The aesthetic is inspired by the legendary Vectrex and early 1980s vector arcade hits like Asteroids or Star Wars.
How it Works
The console utilizes the XY-Mode of an oscilloscope.

    Vector Rendering: Every object is drawn as a sequence of lines. The ESP32 outputs varying voltages (0-3.3V) on two pins to position the beam.
    Simulated Blanking: Since standard oscilloscopes don't always support Z-axis (brightness) modulation, this engine uses high-speed "jumps" between objects to make the transition lines nearly invisible to the human eye.
    Performance: Written in C++ with optimized inline functions and microsecond-level timing to ensure a flicker-free experience on CRT phosphor.

Features

    Integrated Game Engine: Supports dynamic line drawing, geometric shapes, and custom vector fonts.
    Game Modes:
        Vector-Pong: A 2-player classic with smooth paddle movement.
        Vector-Invaders: A shooter featuring procedural enemy movement and 4-tone retro sound effects.
    Custom Vector Text: Includes a specialized library for rendering characters (e.g., "FH KAERNTEN" demo).
    Analog Input: Precise control using two potentiometers.

Hardware & Wiring
To build this, you need an ESP32 (30-pin or 38-pin version) and an analog oscilloscope.
Pin Mapping
Component	ESP32 Pin	Description
DAC X-Axis	GPIO 25	Horizontal Deflection (Scope CH1)
DAC Y-Axis	GPIO 26	Vertical Deflection (Scope CH2)
Potentiometer L	GPIO 34	Player 1 / Menu Selection
Potentiometer R	GPIO 35	Player 2 Control
Fire Button	GPIO 32	Digital Input (Start/Shoot)
Sound Output	GPIO 27	PWM Audio for Buzzer/Speaker
Getting Started

    Scope Setup: Set your oscilloscope to XY-Mode.
    Calibration: Adjust the VOLTS/DIV knobs on both channels to scale the game field to your screen size.
    Upload: Flash the provided code using the Arduino IDE or PlatformIO.
    Wiring: Connect DAC pins directly to BNC probes. Ensure the ESP32 and Scope share a common Ground (GND).

Roadmap

    Implement Z-Axis support for true beam blanking on professional scopes.
    Add physics-based "Asteroids" clone.
    Refine the drawLine algorithm for even higher refresh rates.

Stay retro!

